        "title": "Priority 1 — Pure logic, zero excuse not to test (`lib/format.ts`, `lib/reviewer-label.ts`, `types/rules.ts`, `types/run.ts`)",
        "content": "\nThese have no I/O, no mocking needed, and are exercised on every page render.\n\n**`lib/format.ts`** — 7 exported functions, 0 tests.\n\nEdge cases missed:\n- `shortRunId`: id shorter than 6 chars after stripping hyphens (partial/empty result), id with no hyphens, id that's already short\n- `formatElapsed`: negative ms (clamped to 0?), ms that lands exactly on 60s/3600s boundaries, values >24h\n- `formatCount` / `formatStat`: negative numbers, `0` vs `null` vs `undefined` (the whole point of `formatStat` — it must never confuse 0 with unset)\n- `formatRelative`: boundary at exactly 60s/60m/24h, a `date` in the future relative to `now` (negative seconds)\n- `formatConfidence`: values outside 0–1 (e.g., `1` → `\"1.00\"`, does it round correctly at `0.005`)\n\n```ts\n// lib/format.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport {\n  shortRunId, formatElapsed, formatCount, formatStat,\n  formatClockTime, formatTimestamp, formatRelative, formatConfidence,\n} from \"@/lib/format\";\n\ndescribe(\"shortRunId\", () => {\n  it(\"takes 4 chars, ·, 2 chars from a hyphenated uuid\", () => {\n    expect(shortRunId(\"a41fbc21-c9aa-4444-8888-000000000000\")).toBe(\"a41f·bc\");\n  });\n  it(\"handles an id shorter than 6 chars after stripping hyphens\", () => {\n    expect(shortRunId(\"ab-cd\")).toBe(\"abcd·\");\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"formatElapsed\", () => {\n  it(\"clamps negative ms to 00:00\", () => {\n    expect(formatElapsed(-500)).toBe(\"00:00\");\n  });\n  it(\"switches to h:mm:ss past one hour\", () => {\n    expect(formatElapsed(3_600_000)).toBe(\"1:00:00\");\n  });\n  it(\"stays mm:ss just under one hour\", () => {\n    expect(formatElapsed(3_599_000)).toBe(\"59:59\");\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"formatStat\", () => {\n  it(\"renders 0 as '0', not an em dash\", () => {\n    expect(formatStat(0)).toBe(\"0\");\n  });\n  it(\"renders null/undefined as an em dash\", () => {\n    expect(formatStat(null)).toBe(\"—\");\n    expect(formatStat(undefined)).toBe(\"—\");\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"formatRelative\", () => {\n  it(\"says 'just now' under 60s\", () => {\n    const now = new Date(\"2026-08-06T12:00:00Z\");\n    expect(formatRelative(new Date(\"2026-08-06T11:59:30Z\"), now)).toBe(\"just now\");\n  });\n  it(\"rolls over at exact minute/hour/day boundaries\", () => {\n    const now = new Date(\"2026-08-06T12:00:00Z\");\n    expect(formatRelative(new Date(\"2026-08-06T11:00:00Z\"), now)).toBe(\"60m ago\"); // or 1h — verify intended rounding\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"formatConfidence\", () => {\n  it(\"always shows two decimals\", () => {\n    expect(formatConfidence(1)).toBe(\"1.00\");\n    expect(formatConfidence(0)).toBe(\"0.00\");\n  });\n});\n```\n\n**`lib/reviewer-label.ts`** — the audit trail's display identity resolution, untested:\n\n```ts\n// lib/reviewer-label.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { reviewerLabel } from \"@/lib/reviewer-label\";\n\ndescribe(\"reviewerLabel\", () => {\n  it(\"prefers username over email\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: \"j.rao\", primaryEmailAddress: { emailAddress: \"j@x.com\" } }, \"fallback\")).toBe(\"j.rao\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back to email when username is null\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: null, primaryEmailAddress: { emailAddress: \"j@x.com\" } }, \"fallback\")).toBe(\"j@x.com\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back to the userId when user is null\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel(null, \"user_123\")).toBe(\"user_123\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back to userId when both username and email are missing\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: null, primaryEmailAddress: null }, \"user_123\")).toBe(\"user_123\");\n  });\n  it(\"treats empty-string username as falsy? (verify: '' ?? is truthy in JS)\", () => {\n    // NB: `??` only falls through on null/undefined, so username: \"\" would return \"\".\n    // This is worth a dedicated test to lock in current (possibly surprising) behavior.\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: \"\", primaryEmailAddress: null }, \"user_123\")).toBe(\"\");\n  });\n});\n```\nThat last case is a genuine edge case worth locking down either way — `??` doesn't treat `\"\"` as absent, so a Clerk user with an empty-string username (if that's ever possible) would render blank instead of falling back.\n\n**`types/rules.ts`** — `orderRulesByEvaluation` / `ruleSpecificity` implement the survivorship rule-priority contract described in the file's own comment (\"first match wins\"). This is exactly the kind of ordering logic that silently breaks on refactor and has no test today.\n\n```ts\n// types/rules.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { orderRulesByEvaluation, ruleSpecificity, type SurvivorshipRule } from \"@/types/rules\";\n\nconst rule = (overrides: Partial<SurvivorshipRule>): SurvivorshipRule => ({\n  id: \"r1\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"escalate\", description: \"\", ...overrides,\n});\n\ndescribe(\"ruleSpecificity\", () => {\n  it(\"scores exact field + exact entityType highest\", () => {\n    expect(ruleSpecificity(rule({ field: \"name\", entityType: \"account\" }))).toBe(3);\n  });\n  it(\"scores double wildcard lowest\", () => {\n    expect(ruleSpecificity(rule({ field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\" }))).toBe(0);\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"orderRulesByEvaluation\", () => {\n  it(\"sorts most specific first\", () => {\n    const wildcard = rule({ id: \"wild\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\" });\n    const specific = rule({ id: \"specific\", field: \"name\", entityType: \"account\" });\n    expect(orderRulesByEvaluation([wildcard, specific]).map((r) => r.id)).toEqual([\"specific\", \"wild\"]);\n  });\n  it(\"keeps author order among equal-specificity rules (stable sort, first match wins)\", () => {\n    const a = rule({ id: \"a\", field: \"name\", entityType: \"*\" });\n    const b = rule({ id: \"b\", field: \"phone\", entityType: \"*\" });\n    expect(orderRulesByEvaluation([a, b]).map((r) => r.id)).toEqual([\"a\", \"b\"]);\n  });\n  it(\"does not mutate the input array\", () => {\n    const input = [rule({ id: \"a\" }), rule({ id: \"b\", field: \"x\" })];\n    const copy = [...input];\n    orderRulesByEvaluation(input);\n    expect(input).toEqual(copy);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n**`types/run.ts`** — `isTerminalRunStatus`, `runStageIndex`:\n\n```ts\n// types/run.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { isTerminalRunStatus, runStageIndex } from \"@/types/run\";\n\ndescribe(\"isTerminalRunStatus\", () => {\n  it(\"complete and failed are terminal\", () => {\n    expect(isTerminalRunStatus(\"complete\")).toBe(true);\n    expect(isTerminalRunStatus(\"failed\")).toBe(true);\n  });\n  it(\"every in-progress stage is non-terminal\", () => {\n    for (const s of [\"queued\", \"extracting\", \"matching\", \"detecting\", \"resolving\"] as const) {\n      expect(isTerminalRunStatus(s)).toBe(false);\n    }\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"runStageIndex\", () => {\n  it(\"returns -1 for failed (carries no position, per the doc comment)\", () => {\n    expect(runStageIndex(\"failed\")).toBe(-1);\n  });\n  it(\"returns increasing indices through the pipeline\", () => {\n    expect(runStageIndex(\"queued\")).toBe(0);\n    expect(runStageIndex(\"complete\")).toBe(5);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Priority 2 — Validation boundary (`app/api/_lib/respond.ts`, `types/*.ts` schemas)",
        "content": "\nEvery route handler leans on `parseJson` + `isUuid` for its entire input-validation story. This is untested and it's the literal security boundary (AGENTS.md: \"every route handler validates its body with Zod before touching the database, no exceptions\").\n\n```ts\n// app/api/_lib/respond.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { parseJson, isUuid, badRequest, notFound } from \"@/app/api/_lib/respond\";\n\nconst schema = z.object({ name: z.string().min(1) });\n\nfunction req(body: unknown) {\n  return new Request(\"http://test\", { method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify(body) });\n}\n\ndescribe(\"parseJson\", () => {\n  it(\"returns ok:true with parsed data on a valid body\", async () => {\n    const result = await parseJson(req({ name: \"x\" }), schema);\n    expect(result).toEqual({ ok: true, data: { name: \"x\" } });\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns 400 when the body is not JSON\", async () => {\n    const badReq = new Request(\"http://test\", { method: \"POST\", body: \"not json\" });\n    const result = await parseJson(badReq, schema);\n    expect(result.ok).toBe(false);\n    if (!result.ok) expect(result.response.status).toBe(400);\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns 400 with field-addressable issues on schema violation\", async () => {\n    const result = await parseJson(req({ name: \"\" }), schema);\n    expect(result.ok).toBe(false);\n    if (!result.ok) {\n      const body = await result.response.json();\n      expect(body.issues).toHaveProperty(\"name\");\n    }\n  });\n\n  it(\"addresses nested-path issues with dot notation\", async () => {\n    const nested = z.object({ config: z.object({ objects: z.array(z.string()).min(1) }) });\n    const result = await parseJson(req({ config: { objects: [] } }), nested);\n    expect(result.ok).toBe(false);\n    if (!result.ok) {\n      const body = await result.response.json();\n      expect(body.issues).toHaveProperty(\"config.objects\");\n    }\n  });\n\n  it(\"addresses a root-level issue as '_'\", async () => {\n    // e.g. schema.refine at the top level with no path\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"isUuid\", () => {\n  it(\"accepts a well-formed v4-shaped uuid\", () => {\n    expect(isUuid(\"a41fbc21-c9aa-4444-8888-000000000000\")).toBe(true);\n  });\n  it(\"rejects a non-uuid path param without throwing\", () => {\n    expect(isUuid(\"../../etc/passwd\")).toBe(false);\n    expect(isUuid(\"\")).toBe(false);\n    expect(isUuid(\"not-a-uuid\")).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"is case-insensitive\", () => {\n    expect(isUuid(\"A41FBC21-C9AA-4444-8888-000000000000\")).toBe(true);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n**`types/rules.ts` — `survivorshipRuleSchema.refine`**: the one conditionally-required field (`preferredSourceId` required iff `strategy === \"prefer_source\"`) is exactly the kind of cross-field validation that regresses silently.\n\n```ts\n// types/rules.schema.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { survivorshipRuleSchema } from \"@/types/rules\";\n\ndescribe(\"survivorshipRuleSchema\", () => {\n  it(\"rejects prefer_source without preferredSourceId\", () => {\n    const result = survivorshipRuleSchema.safeParse({\n      id: \"1\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"prefer_source\", description: \"x\",\n    });\n    expect(result.success).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"accepts prefer_source with preferredSourceId\", () => {\n    const result = survivorshipRuleSchema.safeParse({\n      id: \"1\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"prefer_source\",\n      preferredSourceId: \"src_1\", description: \"x\",\n    });\n    expect(result.success).toBe(true);\n  });\n  it(\"does not require preferredSourceId for other strategies\", () => {\n    const result = survivorshipRuleSchema.safeParse({\n      id: \"1\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"escalate\", description: \"x\",\n    });\n    expect(result.success).toBe(true);\n  });\n});\n```\n\nAlso untested: `createSourceSchema`/`sourceConfigSchema` (`objects: z.array(z.string()).min(1)` — empty array rejection), `createRunSchema` (non-uuid ids), `reviewActionSchema`'s discriminated union (`override` requires non-empty `overrideValue`, `approve`/`reject` reject extra fields or not?).\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Priority 3 — Route handlers (`app/api/**/route.ts`) — integration gaps",
        "content": "\nNone of these have any test coverage, and they contain the actual business rules (not just plumbing):\n\n- **`app/api/runs/route.ts` POST** — this is the most logic-dense handler and has zero coverage of:\n  - `sourceAId === sourceBId` → 400 \"must be different systems\"\n  - one or both source ids don't exist → 400 with the specific message per side\n  - ruleset id doesn't exist → 400\n  - happy path: run created, audit `run_started` appended, engine dispatched, 201 with `runId`\n  - **the critical failure path**: `startRun` throws `EngineUnreachableError` → run marked `failed` via `failUndispatchedRun`, a `run_failed` audit entry is appended, and the response is still `201` (not 500) — this dual-outcome behavior is the whole point of wireframe 1h and is currently unverified by anything\n  - a non-`EngineUnreachableError` thrown by `startRun` should propagate/rethrow, not be swallowed\n\n```ts\n// app/api/runs/route.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect, vi, beforeEach } from \"vitest\";\nimport { POST } from \"@/app/api/runs/route\";\n\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/auth\", () => ({ requireReviewer: vi.fn() }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/audit\", () => ({ appendAudit: vi.fn() }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/engine\", async (importOriginal) => ({\n  ...(await importOriginal<object>()),\n  startRun: vi.fn(),\n}));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/runs\", () => ({\n  createRun: vi.fn(),\n  failUndispatchedRun: vi.fn(),\n  listRuns: vi.fn(),\n}));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/rulesets\", () => ({ getRuleset: vi.fn() }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/sources\", () => ({ findSourceIds: vi.fn() }));\n\nfunction postReq(body: unknown) {\n  return new Request(\"http://test/api/runs\", { method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify(body) });\n}\n\ndescribe(\"POST /api/runs\", () => {\n  it(\"400s when sourceAId equals sourceBId\", async () => {\n    const res = await POST(postReq({ sourceAId: SAME, sourceBId: SAME, rulesetId: R }));\n    expect(res.status).toBe(400);\n  });\n\n  it(\"400s when sourceAId does not exist\", async () => {\n    // mock findSourceIds to return a set missing sourceAId\n    // expect res.status === 400 and message mentions \"Source A\"\n  });\n\n  it(\"marks the run failed but still returns 201 when the engine is unreachable\", async () => {\n    // mock createRun to resolve, startRun to reject with EngineUnreachableError\n    // assert failUndispatchedRun was called with the run id\n    // assert appendAudit was called with action: \"run_failed\"\n    // assert response.status === 201\n  });\n\n  it(\"rethrows non-EngineUnreachableError failures from startRun\", async () => {\n    // startRun rejects with a plain Error → the handler should not catch it\n  });\n\n  it(\"resolves the reviewer from the session, never from the request body\", async () => {\n    // assert appendAudit's actor comes from requireReviewer()'s label,\n    // even if the POST body somehow included an actor-like field\n  });\n});\n```\n\n- **`app/api/runs/[runId]/status/route.ts`** — untested branch coverage:\n  - non-uuid `runId` → 404 without hitting the DB\n  - unknown-but-valid uuid → 404\n  - **terminal run status skips the engine call entirely** (returns row data, `fetchEngineStatus` must not be called) — this is a meaningful behavior worth asserting the mock wasn't invoked\n  - non-terminal run + engine reachable → engine's live status wins\n  - non-terminal run + engine unreachable (`fetchEngineStatus` returns `null`) → falls back to the row\n  - response always sets `cache-control: no-store`\n\n- **`app/api/runs/[runId]/route.ts`** and **`app/api/sources/route.ts`** and **`app/api/rulesets/route.ts`** — currently zero coverage of the `isUuid` 404 guard, the \"found\" happy path, and (for `POST`) the `parseJson` failure → 400 passthrough.\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Priority 4 — `lib/server/*.ts` (DB access layer) — integration test gap",
        "content": "\nThis entire layer (`sources.ts`, `runs.ts`, `rulesets.ts`, `audit.ts`, `engine.ts`, `auth.ts`) has **no tests of any kind**, and it's where the trickiest logic lives:\n\n- **`rulesets.ts` `createRulesetVersion`** — this is the highest-risk untested function in the codebase: it uses `pg_advisory_xact_lock` + a transaction to guarantee two concurrent \"save as vN\" calls for the same ruleset name can't both compute the same next version. This needs an integration test against a real (or dockerized) Postgres, not mocks — the guarantee is specifically about transaction/lock semantics that a mock can't represent. Also untested: first version ever created for a name (`highest ?? 0) + 1` → should be `1`), and that saving a new version deactivates the previously-active one.\n\n- **`runs.ts` `failUndispatchedRun`** — the `WHERE status = 'queued'` guard is the entire point of the function (a late dispatch error must not overwrite real engine progress) and is completely unverified. Needs a test that seeds a run already past `queued` and confirms the update is a no-op.\n\n- **`sources.ts` `findSourceIds`** — the `ids.length === 0` short-circuit avoids a query; untested. Also untested: partial matches (some ids exist, some don't) returning only the found subset.\n\n- **`engine.ts`** — the HTTP client to the reconciliation engine has rich, entirely-untested error handling:\n  - `engineUrl()` throws `EngineUnreachableError` when `ENGINE_URL` is unset\n  - trailing-slash stripping in `engineUrl()`\n  - `startRun`: fetch throws (network error) → wrapped in `EngineUnreachableError`\n  - `startRun`: `AbortSignal.timeout` fires → `describe()` special-cases `TimeoutError` into a friendlier message — untested\n  - `startRun`: response not `ok` → error includes truncated body (300 chars) via `readBody`\n  - `startRun`: response `ok` but body doesn't match `engineStartRunResponseSchema` → `EngineUnreachableError` (\"contract break\")\n  - `fetchEngineStatus`: any thrown error → swallowed, returns `null` (not rethrown — this is a deliberate behavior per the comment and deserves a lock-in test)\n  - `fetchEngineStatus`: non-ok response → `null`\n  - `fetchEngineStatus`: body fails schema validation → `null`\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/engine.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect, vi, beforeEach, afterEach } from \"vitest\";\nimport { startRun, fetchEngineStatus, EngineUnreachableError } from \"@/lib/server/engine\";\n\nconst RUN_ID = \"a41fbc21-c9aa-4444-8888-000000000000\";\n\ndescribe(\"startRun\", () => {\n  beforeEach(() => { process.env.ENGINE_URL = \"http://engine.local\"; });\n  afterEach(() => { vi.restoreAllMocks(); delete process.env.ENGINE_URL; });\n\n  it(\"throws EngineUnreachableError when ENGINE_URL is unset\", async () => {\n    delete process.env.ENGINE_URL;\n    await expect(startRun(RUN_ID)).rejects.toThrow(EngineUnreachableError);\n  });\n\n  it(\"wraps a network failure\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockRejectedValue(new Error(\"ECONNREFUSED\")));\n    await expect(startRun(RUN_ID)).rejects.toThrow(EngineUnreachableError);\n  });\n\n  it(\"wraps a non-ok response with the truncated body\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(\n      new Response(\"boom\".repeat(200), { status: 500 }),\n    ));\n    await expect(startRun(RUN_ID)).rejects.toThrow(/500/);\n  });\n\n  it(\"throws a contract-break error when the 200 body doesn't match the schema\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(\n      Response.json({ unexpected: true }),\n    ));\n    await expect(startRun(RUN_ID)).rejects.toThrow(/contract/i);\n  });\n\n  it(\"succeeds on a well-formed accepted response\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(\n      Response.json({ runId: RUN_ID, accepted: true }),\n    ));\n    await expect(startRun(RUN_ID)).resolves.toBeUndefined();\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"fetchEngineStatus\", () => {\n  it(\"returns null (not throw) when fetch rejects\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockRejectedValue(new Error(\"down\")));\n    await expect(fetchEngineStatus(RUN_ID)).resolves.toBeNull();\n  });\n  it(\"returns null on a non-ok response\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(new Response(null, { status: 503 })));\n    await expect(fetchEngineStatus(RUN_ID)).resolves.toBeNull();\n  });\n  it(\"returns null when the body fails schema validation\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(Response.json({ garbage: true })));\n    await expect(fetchEngineStatus(RUN_ID)).resolves.toBeNull();\n  });\n});\n```\n\n- **`auth.ts` `requireReviewer`** — untested: throws when `isAuthenticated` is false, throws when `userId` is missing despite `isAuthenticated` (defensive branch, currently unreachable-by-design but should be locked in), and that `getReviewer` returns `null` rather than throwing when signed out (vs. `requireReviewer`'s throw — these two functions' differing contracts are the entire reason both exist and neither is tested).\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Priority 5 — Components (React) — currently 0% covered",
        "content": "\nLower risk (mostly presentational) but two are worth calling out because they encode actual business rules, not just markup:\n\n- **`SeverityMark`** (`components/severity-mark.tsx`) — AGENTS.md explicitly requires severity is \"NEVER rendered without the word\" (no color-only encoding). A snapshot/RTL test that asserts both the mark glyph *and* the text label are present for all four severities would catch a future refactor that silently drops the accessibility-driving text.\n  ```tsx\n  // components/severity-mark.test.tsx\n  import { render, screen } from \"@testing-library/react\";\n  import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\n  import { SeverityMark } from \"@/components/severity-mark\";\n\n  describe(\"SeverityMark\", () => {\n    it.each([4, 3, 2, 1] as const)(\"renders both the glyph and the word for severity %i\", (severity) => {\n      render(<SeverityMark severity={severity} />);\n      expect(screen.getByText(/structural|attribute|cosmetic/i)).toBeInTheDocument();\n    });\n  });\n  ```\n\n- **`SourceTable`** (`components/sources/source-table.tsx`) — untested: empty `sources` array renders a header with no rows (not a crash), a source with an empty `config.objects` (schema requires `min(1)` so shouldn't happen, but the `.join(\", \")` call is unguarded against it reaching the component with bad data from a stale cache), multiple objects join correctly.\n\nAll other components (`app/page.tsx`, `get-started/*`, `landing/*`, `console/*`, `auth/*`) are pure layout/marketing with no conditional logic worth unit testing — reasonable to leave uncovered or cover with a handful of smoke renders, not a priority.\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Summary table",
        "content": "| Area | Functions/handlers | Current coverage | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `lib/format.ts` | 7 pure functions | 0% | P1 |\n| `lib/reviewer-label.ts` | 1 pure function | 0% | P1 |\n| `types/rules.ts` | ordering + schema refine | 0% | P1 |\n| `types/run.ts` | 2 pure functions | 0% | P1 |\n| `app/api/_lib/respond.ts` | validation plumbing | 0% | P2 |\n| `types/*.ts` zod schemas | ~10 schemas | 0% | P2 |\n| `app/api/runs/route.ts` POST | dispatch + failure recording | 0% | P3 |\n| `app/api/runs/[runId]/status/route.ts` | engine/row fallback | 0% | P3 |\n| other route handlers | CRUD + 404/400 guards | 0% | P3 |\n| `lib/server/rulesets.ts` | advisory-lock versioning | 0% | **P4 — highest concurrency risk** |\n| `lib/server/engine.ts` | HTTP client + error taxonomy | 0% | P4 |\n| `lib/server/runs.ts`, `sources.ts`, `audit.ts`, `auth.ts` | DB access | 0% | P4 |\n| Components | `SeverityMark`, `SourceTable` | 0% | P5 |\n\n**Suggested order of attack**: install Vitest → P1 (an afternoon, no mocking) → P2 (schema/validation, still no mocking) → P4's `rulesets.ts` and `engine.ts` (highest-value, currently-invisible bugs would live here) → P3 (route handlers, needs the P4 mocks anyway) → P5.",
        "level": 3
      }
    ],
    "codeBlocks": [
      {
        "language": "bash",
        "code": "npm i -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/jest-dom @testing-library/user-event"
      },
